    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Coptic is the last stage of Egyptian, a language whose recorded history may be the longest of any, written not in hieroglyphs or Demotic but in Greek letters with seven signs taken from Demotic.</li><li>Christian from its first literary texts in the 3rd century, it was the language of the saints and monks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>; Sahidic was its great literary dialect, and Shenoute’s sermons helped to standardise it.</li><li>After the Arab conquest Arabic replaced it, in government at the turn of the 8th century and later in writing and speech; Coptic survives in the liturgy of the Coptic churches and in borrowed words such as adobe and barge.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The word adobe goes back to Coptic: Sahidic tōōbe, “brick”, became Arabic ṭūba, passed through Andalusi Arabic into Spanish as adobe, and from Spanish into American English.</p></aside>
    </section>

